<article class="env-lemma" id="07PG">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07PG">Lemma <span data-tag="07PG">15.49.4</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a regular ring. Let $f \in R$. Assume there exists a derivation $D : R \to R$ such that $D(f)$ is a unit of $R$. Then $R[z]/(z^ n - f)$ is regular for any integer $n \geq 1$. More generally, $R[z]/(p(z) - f)$ is regular for any $p \in \mathbf{Z}[z]$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/07NF" data-tag="07NF">10.163.10</a> we see that $R[z]$ is a regular ring. Apply Lemma <a href="/tag/07PF" data-tag="07PF">15.49.2</a> to the extension of $D$ to $R[z]$ which maps $z$ to zero. This works because $D$ annihilates any polynomial with integer coefficients and sends $f$ to a unit. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>